These are easily proved using the denition of . For example, x1 x2 ; y1 y2 =
(x1 ; y1 )x2 + y1 (x2 ; y2 ) is a sum of two multiples of n.
   Equation (3.1) says that it is meaningful to add, subtract or multiply two
congruence classes, since the equivalence class of the result does not depend
upon the selections of the representatives. The congruence classes modulo n
form a commutative ring under these operations. This ring, denoted by =n ,  Z   Z

is called integers modulo n. When n is prime, division by nonzero elements
is possible, and this ring is a eld, often written GF(n).
