3.3. Chinese remainder theorem
Let n1 and n2 be coprime positive integers. If r1 and r2 are arbitrary integers,
then the two congruences

                                                340
    x  r1 (mod n1 )        and      x  r2 (mod n2 )                        (3.3)
may be replaced by a single congruence
    x  r (mod n1 n2 )                                              (3.4)
where r is chosen to satisfy r  r1 (mod n1 ) and r  r2 (mod n2 ). This is
known as the Chinese Remainder Theorem.
  For example, the two congruences
    x  3 (mod 13)         and      x  9 (mod 17)                           (3.5)
are equivalent to the single congruence x  94 (mod 221). To conrm this,
note rst that 1 = 52 ; 51 = 4  13 ; 3  17 such an equation exists since 13
and 17 are coprime (cf. (4.1)). If (3.5) holds, then there exist integers k1 and
k2 such that x = 13k1 + 3 = 17k2 + 9. Hence
        x = 52x ; 51x = 52(17k2 + 9) ; 51(13k1 + 3)
           = 884k2 ; 663k1 + 315 = 221(4k2 ; 3k1 + 1) + 94
which shows that x  94 (mod 221). Conversely, if x  94 (mod 221), say
x = 221k + 94, then x = 13(17k + 7) + 3 = 17(13k + 5) + 9, implying (3.5).
   A generalization allows more than two moduli. If gcd(ni  nj ) = 1 for 1 
